---
// Recognition section for professionals — the pain points, in order of how
// often practitioners say them out loud: insight that doesn't move anything
// (named mechanism: the block is held in the body/nervous system, not the
// story), the session clock, the pre-verbal ceiling, the moment real emotion
// arrives (overwhelm/shutdown), and what stays in the practitioner's own
// body. The accent quote is Jacob's own (copy book §2); the pull line is
// copy book B.12.
---

<section class="we-opening">
  <div class="we-opening-inner">
    <div class="we-eyebrow">You know this moment</div>
    <h2 class="we-opening-head">
      Your clients understand their block,<br />
      and yet <em>nothing moves.</em>
    </h2>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      If you work with people — in a practice, a classroom, a session room, a
      team — you know the moment. The pattern has been named. The story has
      been told twice — and a story is always curated: in words, people show
      you what they've decided to show. The insight is accurate, and it
      changes nothing, because the block doesn't live where insight lives.
      It's held in the body, in the nervous system, below the reach of
      explanation. The hour ends where it ended last time.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      You know the other versions too. The mixed feeling that takes ten
      minutes to set up in words — words go single file, and the clock runs.
      The earliest material, laid down before language, that no conversation
      can reach: anyone working near attachment or the first years knows that
      ceiling. And the moment nobody mentions at conferences: real emotion
      finally arrives in the room — the person in front of you tips into
      overwhelm, or drops into shutdown — and the honest inner question is
      <em>what do I do now?</em>
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      Then there's the quieter version, the one about you. You hold other
      people's hardest hours all week — your own nervous system in the room
      for every one of them — and some of what they bring stays in your body
      after the door closes. Most professionals have no practice where that
      ever gets heard.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      This masterclass is about exactly those moments. In Somatic Vocal
      Healing the question isn't <em>tell me more about it</em> — it's
      <em>what is the sound of it?</em> Sound before story: the feeling words
      would have to circle arrives whole, in one honest tone — and the person
      in front of you was born knowing how to make it.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      It doesn't replace what you do. It works underneath it. Talking is
      top-down — it starts from the story. Sounding is bottom-up — it starts
      from the body, at the level where the block is actually held.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-accent">
      "I've never had a therapist ask me: <em>could you make a sound for
      that?</em> It's odd and sad that it doesn't happen — because it's still
      so natural to us." — Jacob
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-pull">
      Talking circles it.<br />Sounding enters it.
    </p>
    <a href="#register" class="we-link-cta">
      Reserve your seat <i class="ph-light ph-arrow-right"></i>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
